          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "If a record says \"diabetes with neuropathy\", does the coder need a statement that the diabetes caused the neuropathy?",
          "answer": "**No, not for the pairings the index lists.** Under the ICD-10-CM \"with\" convention, when the Alphabetic Index links two conditions with the word \"with,\" the classification reads that as \"associated with\" or \"due to\" and presumes the link. The record does not need to state the cause. The coding guidance gives the example of type 2 diabetes and chronic kidney disease, which is coded as diabetic kidney disease unless the documentation says the two are unrelated (*Coding type 2 diabetes: E11 and its complication subcategories*).\n\nThe same logic applies to neuropathy. Diabetic neuropathy is coded from the diabetes category in the endocrine chapter, not from the polyneuropathy block, because the classification presumes the association (*ICD-10-CM Chapter 6: diseases of the nervous system*).\n\nTwo limits apply:\n- The presumption holds only for pairings the index actually lists. It does not extend to any two conditions that appear together.\n- It is overridden when the documentation says the two are unrelated (*ICD-10-CM code structure and the conventions that govern selection*).\n\nThe passages I retrieved do not include the full index entries or the official coding guidelines. For the exact rule for a specific code, a coder should check the current official ICD-10-CM index and guidelines.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
